WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.374
Lesson 5 becomes much clearer when rms values and power transmission are treated as one chain: meaningful a.c.

2
00:00:06.374 --> 00:00:12.747
readings lead into meaningful loss calculations.

3
00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:19.121
RMS values means heating-equivalent readings.

4
00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:25.495
They let an alternating supply be compared to a d.c.

5
00:00:25.495 --> 00:00:31.868
supply in terms of heating effect.

6
00:00:31.868 --> 00:00:38.242
Power link means current depends on voltage.

7
00:00:38.242 --> 00:00:44.615
For a given delivered power, current is smaller when the transmission voltage is larger.

8
00:00:44.615 --> 00:00:50.989
Loss link means I squared R.

9
00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:57.363
Cable heating depends very strongly on the current.

10
00:00:57.363 --> 00:01:03.736
RMS readings and high-voltage transmission belong on the same practical power-delivery route.

11
00:01:03.736 --> 00:01:10.110
Keep this takeaway in view: RMS readings and high-voltage transmission belong on the same practical power-delivery route.

12
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:15.474
This diagram matters because it keeps the meaning of the rms reading tied to the current and loss consequences in the transmission line.

13
00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:20.839
Wave size means peak and rms.

14
00:01:20.839 --> 00:01:26.203
The alternating supply still needs a practical way to report its effect.

15
00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:31.568
Current route means I = P / V.

16
00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:36.932
Delivered power and transmission voltage fix the line current.

17
00:01:36.932 --> 00:01:42.297
Loss route means P_loss = I^2 R.

18
00:01:42.297 --> 00:01:47.661
Once the current is known, cable heating follows immediately.

19
00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:53.026
The practical reading and the practical loss story belong together.

20
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:58.390
Keep this takeaway in view: The practical reading and the practical loss story belong together.

21
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.721
An effort-versus-flow analogy helps because transmission is really about delivering the same power while reducing the burden carried by the current in the cables.

22
00:02:03.721 --> 00:02:09.052
Delivered job means fixed power target.

23
00:02:09.052 --> 00:02:14.383
The amount of power reaching the destination is the quantity being held steady.

24
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:19.714
Higher push means higher voltage.

25
00:02:19.714 --> 00:02:25.046
A larger voltage means the same power can be carried with less current.

26
00:02:25.046 --> 00:02:30.377
Lower route stress means smaller current loss.

27
00:02:30.377 --> 00:02:35.708
Smaller current means much less heating in the cables.

28
00:02:35.708 --> 00:02:41.039
High-voltage transmission works because it lowers the current needed for the same power.

29
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:46.370
Keep this takeaway in view: High-voltage transmission works because it lowers the current needed for the same power.

30
00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:53.465
Lesson 5 is strongest when the learner treats the rms relation, the power relation, and the loss relation as consecutive steps in one chain.

31
00:02:53.465 --> 00:03:00.559
RMS job means equivalent heating effect.

32
00:03:00.559 --> 00:03:07.654
The rms value is the d.c.-equivalent reading for the same heating effect.

33
00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:14.748
Current job means depends on power and voltage.

34
00:03:14.748 --> 00:03:21.843
At fixed power, larger voltage means smaller current.

35
00:03:21.843 --> 00:03:28.937
Loss job means depends on current squared.

36
00:03:28.937 --> 00:03:36.032
Small drops in current create large drops in heating loss.

37
00:03:36.032 --> 00:03:43.126
Keep these relations visible: V_rms = V_peak / sqrt(2); I_rms = V_rms / R for a resistor; P = V I; and P_loss = I^2 R.

38
00:03:43.126 --> 00:03:50.221
Use rms values when talking about practical readings and heating effect, then use the current found from the power relation in the cable-loss calculation.

39
00:03:50.221 --> 00:03:57.315
Lesson 5 works best as one linked quantitative chain.

40
00:03:57.315 --> 00:04:04.410
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 works best as one linked quantitative chain.

41
00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:09.754
A dependable Lesson 5 route starts by holding the delivered power fixed, then changing only the transmission voltage, then recalculating the current and the cable loss.

42
00:04:09.754 --> 00:04:15.099
Step 1 means fix the delivered power.

43
00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:20.443
Keep the power target the same in both comparisons.

44
00:04:20.443 --> 00:04:25.788
Step 2 means find the line current.

45
00:04:25.788 --> 00:04:31.132
Use I = P / V for each voltage choice.

46
00:04:31.132 --> 00:04:36.477
Step 3 means find the cable loss.

47
00:04:36.477 --> 00:04:41.821
Use P_loss = I^2 R and compare the results.

48
00:04:41.821 --> 00:04:47.166
Transmission comparisons become easy once current is treated as the hinge quantity.

49
00:04:47.166 --> 00:04:52.510
Keep this takeaway in view: Transmission comparisons become easy once current is treated as the hinge quantity.

50
00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:56.772
The first deep idea is the meaning of rms.

51
00:04:56.772 --> 00:05:01.034
It is not an average wave height.

52
00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:05.295
It is the steady-value comparison that matches the heating effect in a resistor.

53
00:05:05.295 --> 00:05:09.557
RMS meaning means d.c.-equivalent heating.

54
00:05:09.557 --> 00:05:13.819
This is what gives the reading physical value.

55
00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:18.081
Peak value means larger than rms.

56
00:05:18.081 --> 00:05:22.343
The wave reaches the peak only momentarily.

57
00:05:22.343 --> 00:05:26.605
Lesson gain means readings make sense.

58
00:05:26.605 --> 00:05:30.866
The practical supply value is connected to what the circuit actually experiences thermally.

59
00:05:30.866 --> 00:05:35.128
Protect the heating-equivalence meaning whenever rms appears.

60
00:05:35.128 --> 00:05:39.390
Keep this takeaway in view: Protect the heating-equivalence meaning whenever rms appears.

61
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:45.039
A peak-to-rms conversion matters because many questions describe the wave by its peak size and then expect a practical supply reading or resistor current.

62
00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:50.688
Voltage bridge means V_rms = V_peak / sqrt(2).

63
00:05:50.688 --> 00:05:56.337
This converts the sinusoidal peak voltage into the practical rms reading.

64
00:05:56.337 --> 00:06:01.986
Current bridge means same pattern for current.

65
00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:07.634
The same square-root-of-two bridge holds for sinusoidal current.

66
00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:13.283
Use case means resistive calculations.

67
00:06:13.283 --> 00:06:18.932
Rms values are the right values for heating and power calculations in these contexts.

68
00:06:18.932 --> 00:06:24.581
Use the rms bridge before doing practical resistor or supply calculations.

69
00:06:24.581 --> 00:06:30.230
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the rms bridge before doing practical resistor or supply calculations.

70
00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:35.012
The key transmission idea is that the delivered power target can stay the same while the current falls when the voltage is raised.

71
00:06:35.012 --> 00:06:39.794
Fixed target means same delivered power.

72
00:06:39.794 --> 00:06:44.577
The comparison keeps the power being sent unchanged.

73
00:06:44.577 --> 00:06:49.359
Higher voltage means lower current.

74
00:06:49.359 --> 00:06:54.141
Because I = P / V, raising V lowers I.

75
00:06:54.141 --> 00:06:58.923
Lesson gain means the slogan becomes quantitative.

76
00:06:58.923 --> 00:07:03.706
High-voltage transmission is a calculation, not a catchphrase.

77
00:07:03.706 --> 00:07:08.488
For the same power, higher transmission voltage means lower current.

78
00:07:08.488 --> 00:07:13.270
Keep this takeaway in view: For the same power, higher transmission voltage means lower current.

79
00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:18.611
The final transmission bridge is the loss calculation.

80
00:07:18.611 --> 00:07:23.952
Once the current falls, the heating loss falls much more sharply because the current is squared in the loss relation.

81
00:07:23.952 --> 00:07:29.293
Loss formula means P_loss = I^2 R.

82
00:07:29.293 --> 00:07:34.634
This is the route to cable heating.

83
00:07:34.634 --> 00:07:39.975
Big payoff means small current means much smaller loss.

84
00:07:39.975 --> 00:07:45.316
Squaring the current magnifies the benefit of reducing it.

85
00:07:45.316 --> 00:07:50.657
Practical result means greater transmission efficiency.

86
00:07:50.657 --> 00:07:55.998
More of the generated power reaches the destination instead of heating the line.

87
00:07:55.998 --> 00:08:01.339
The I squared dependence is why voltage stepping matters so much in transmission.

88
00:08:01.339 --> 00:08:06.680
Keep this takeaway in view: The I squared dependence is why voltage stepping matters so much in transmission.

89
00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.853
Use this checkpoint to test whether the learner has connected the power relation to the cable-loss relation in the right order.

90
00:08:08.853 --> 00:08:11.027
Question means higher transmission voltage.

91
00:08:11.027 --> 00:08:13.200
What should happen to the line current and the cable heating loss?

92
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.373
Route means current falls first.

93
00:08:15.373 --> 00:08:17.547
Use I = P / V before thinking about the loss.

94
00:08:17.547 --> 00:08:19.720
Answer check means loss falls sharply.

95
00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.893
Because current is smaller, the I squared R loss becomes much smaller too.

96
00:08:21.893 --> 00:08:24.067
Higher voltage lowers current, and lower current sharply lowers line loss.

97
00:08:24.067 --> 00:08:26.240
Keep this takeaway in view: Higher voltage lowers current, and lower current sharply lowers line loss.

98
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:32.185
Carry one route into the worked example.

99
00:08:32.185 --> 00:08:38.130
Convert peak values to rms when needed, use power and voltage to find the transmission current, then use the cable resistance and I squared R to compare the losses.

100
00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:44.075
Step 1 means rms if needed.

101
00:08:44.075 --> 00:08:50.020
Use the practical reading first.

102
00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:55.965
Step 2 means current.

103
00:08:55.965 --> 00:09:01.910
Find current from the delivered power and voltage.

104
00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:07.855
Step 3 means loss.

105
00:09:07.855 --> 00:09:13.800
Use that current in I squared R.

106
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:19.745
Keep these relations visible: convert peak to rms when the question gives peak values; use P = V I to find the current; and use P_loss = I^2 R to compare cable losses.

107
00:09:19.745 --> 00:09:25.690
The current should be treated as the hinge quantity between the practical supply reading and the transmission-loss calculation.

108
00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:31.635
Lesson 5 questions become straightforward when the chain is followed in order.

109
00:09:31.635 --> 00:09:37.580
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 questions become straightforward when the chain is followed in order.
